# <%= siteName %>

A site that is prerendered at build time: `outputMode: "static"` renders every
route to its own HTML file, and a static host serves those files. There is no
server, so nothing has to run between a request and the page.

Its scripts are in `package.json` beside this file. From the workspace root:
<% const width = Math.max(...['start', 'build', 'e2e'].map((verb) => run(verb).length)) + 2; %>
```bash
<%= run('start').padEnd(width) %># http://localhost:<%= port %>
<%= run('build').padEnd(width) %># dist/<%= name %>/browser, then the checks below
<%= run('test') %><% if (e2e) { %>
<%= run('e2e').padEnd(width) %># hydration, the 404 page, and AXE on every page in both colour schemes<% } %>
```

## Pages

Each route in `src/app/app.routes.ts` carries `data.seo`: a title, and a
description unique across the site. `PageMetaStrategy`
(`src/app/seo/page-meta.ts`) writes the title, description, canonical, robots,
Open Graph and JSON-LD tags from it on every navigation. That includes
navigations during prerender, so the tags end up in the static HTML that
crawlers read.

A new page also needs a link from an existing one. Crawlers find pages that
way, and so does the e2e accessibility check.

## After the build

The `build` script runs `postbuild` afterwards, which runs two commands of
`@angular-capacitor-workspace/cli`, the generator this workspace keeps
installed:

1. `sitemap` writes `sitemap.xml` from the prerendered pages, using each
   page's canonical and skipping noindex pages. The sitemap is built from the
   output, so it cannot fall out of step with it.
2. `verify-prerender` fails the build when a page has no `<h1>`
   (a prerender that fell back to an empty shell), a title or description that
   another page also uses, a canonical pointing somewhere else, or a
   `data-theme` pinned on `<html>`. It also fails when the sitemap or
   robots.txt does not match the pages.

## The origin

Canonical URLs are absolute. The origin is `SITE_ORIGIN` in `src/app/site.ts`,
and the `Sitemap:` line in `public/robots.txt` repeats it. The postbuild check
fails the build if the two disagree<% if (placeholderOrigin) { %>, and warns on
every build while the origin is still the example.com placeholder<% } %>.

## Hosting

Upload `dist/<%= name %>/browser` to any static host. A few settings decide
whether search engines treat the site correctly:

- **Real 404s.** Serve `404/index.html` with status 404 for any path that has
  no file of its own. Do not rewrite unknown paths to `/index.html`, which is
  the usual single-page-app setting. That turns every dead link into a 200
  page, and search engines report those as soft 404s.
- **`index.csr.html`** is Angular's client-side rendering fallback. A fully
  prerendered site never serves it, so it can be left out of the upload.
- **Upload order.** Upload the hashed files (`*.js`, `*.css`, `media/`) first
  and never delete them, so a tab that is still open can load the chunks it was
  built against. Then upload the HTML, `sitemap.xml` and `robots.txt`, deleting
  whatever is gone.
- **Caching.** Give hashed files a long, immutable cache lifetime and HTML a
  short one.

## Scale

Every route is prerendered, and a route with parameters lists its values in
`getPrerenderParams` in `app.routes.server.ts`. That works for hundreds of
pages. At tens of thousands, build time and output size become the problem;
those routes should then move to `RenderMode.Server`, which needs a host that
can run a server.
